course: dictionary
lesson: 15829
title: Formal words: undergo
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Undergo is a formal word. It means to go through or experience something, often something unpleasant, difficult or important. «My grandad will undergo an operation on his hip next week.» The operation is something that happens to him, and he has to go through it.
explain: You can also undergo a test or a change. «The caterpillar will undergo a change into a butterfly.» The caterpillar does not choose to stop being a caterpillar. It goes through the change because that is what happens to it.
explain: Undergo is a verb, so its form changes with time: undergo now, underwent in the past, and undergone or undergoing with other forms. When you read it, swap in the plain words go through or have. If the sentence still makes sense, you have found its meaning.
fact: Undergo means to go through or experience something, especially something unpleasant or difficult.
fact: «Undergo» is a formal word, so you will often see it in books, letters and tests.
fact: You can undergo an operation, a test or a change.
fact: Plain words for «undergo» are go through and have.
fact: The past tense of «undergo» is «underwent», as in «She underwent surgery last year».
exercise: «The patient will undergo surgery on Monday.» What does undergo mean here? | Watch from a distance | Forget about | *Go through | Run away from
exercise: Which word means the same as undergo in «Each pupil must undergo a short test at the end of term»? | *Go through | Cancel | Skip | Admire
exercise: The metal must ___ heating before it can be shaped. Which word fits best? | forget | avoid | cancel | *undergo
test: Which plain English words can replace undergo in «He will undergo a check-up»? | Shout and run | Hide and wait | *Go through and have | Forget and skip
test: «The river will undergo a great change after the flood.» Does undergo mean stay the same or go through a change? | Stay the same | Stop completely | *Go through a change | Run away from it
exam: Which is the past tense of undergo, as in «Last year she ___ surgery»? | undergoing | undergoes | undergo | *underwent
